WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.884
Two meetings in one room clash, and the calendar must flag it. Interval problems are exactly this, and sorting by start time solves most of them.

00:00:12.084 --> 00:00:13.013
Pause and think.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:16.768
After sorting by start, when do two intervals overlap?

00:00:17.168 --> 00:00:24.482
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:25.682 --> 00:00:31.708
This lesson covers intervals: meetings, reservations, memory ranges and time windows.

00:00:33.058 --> 00:00:35.681
Think of a shared calendar for a meeting room.

00:00:36.131 --> 00:00:39.127
Each meeting is a bar with a start and an end.

00:00:39.577 --> 00:00:42.804
Two bars overlap if each one starts before the other ends.

00:00:43.254 --> 00:00:49.826
Most interval problems become easy once the bars are sorted by start time, because then you only compare neighbors.

00:00:51.176 --> 00:00:54.949
Merge intervals: sort by start, then sweep left to right.

00:00:55.399 --> 00:01:02.690
The first bar is one to three. The next, two to six, starts before three ends, so merge them into one to six.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.704
Eight to ten starts after six, so it begins a new group.

00:01:07.154 --> 00:01:10.301
One pass after sorting: n log n total.

00:01:11.651 --> 00:01:14.263
How many rooms do we need at the busiest moment?

00:01:14.713 --> 00:01:19.136
Sort meetings by start. Keep a min heap of end times for rooms in use.

00:01:19.182 --> 00:01:31.558
New word: heap. A tree-shaped structure that always gives you the smallest (or largest) item quickly; also the dynamic memory area. Picture it like this: A tournament where the champion is always on top.

00:01:33.208 --> 00:01:39.071
When a new meeting starts after the earliest end on top of the heap, that room is free: pop it.

00:01:39.521 --> 00:01:44.026
Push the new end time. The biggest the heap ever gets is the number of rooms.

00:01:45.376 --> 00:01:46.374
Vocabulary.

00:01:46.824 --> 00:01:56.797
Half open, written with a square bracket and a round bracket, means the end time is not included. A meeting ending at five and one starting at five do not clash.

00:01:57.247 --> 00:02:04.631
A sweep line walks through all start and end events in time order, adding one at a start and subtracting one at an end.

00:02:05.081 --> 00:02:10.503
An interval map, like a balanced tree keyed by start, answers can I book this in log n time.

00:02:11.853 --> 00:02:13.223
Systems uses.

00:02:13.673 --> 00:02:17.690
GPU cluster schedulers reserve nodes over time windows.

00:02:17.841 --> 00:02:31.355
New word: G P U. Graphics Processing Unit: a chip with thousands of small cores that do the same math on lots of data at once. Picture it like this: A stadium of students each solving one simple sum at the same time.

00:02:31.755 --> 00:02:42.053
New word: scheduler. The OS part that decides which task runs on which CPU and when. Picture it like this: A teacher deciding whose turn it is.

00:02:42.453 --> 00:02:52.101
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:02:53.751 --> 00:02:58.987
Memory allocators merge adjacent free ranges, just like merging DMA regions.

00:02:59.051 --> 00:03:12.611
New word: D M A. Direct memory access: a hardware helper that copies data to memory without the processor doing each byte. Picture it like this: A conveyor belt that moves boxes while the worker does something else.

00:03:14.261 --> 00:03:18.464
And monitoring systems compute busy time from start and stop events.

00:03:19.814 --> 00:03:27.279
Sort by start, compare neighbors, and use a heap or sweep line for peaks. Try Merge Intervals and Meeting Rooms Two.

00:03:27.278 --> 00:03:28.532
Back to the question.

00:03:28.932 --> 00:03:32.287
After sorting by start, when do two intervals overlap?

00:03:32.687 --> 00:03:35.474
When the next one starts before the current one ends.

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:37.544
Quick recap.

00:03:37.944 --> 00:03:39.279
Sort by start first.

00:03:39.679 --> 00:03:41.119
Merge while they overlap.

00:03:41.519 --> 00:03:43.284
A heap tracks rooms in use.
